import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"How mlopt works — syntax, structure, and the rewrite loop","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/explanation/mechanism.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/explanation/mechanism.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/explanation/mechanism.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="how-mlopt-works-—-syntax-structure-and-the-rewrite-loop" tabindex="-1">How mlopt works — syntax, structure, and the rewrite loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-mlopt-works-—-syntax-structure-and-the-rewrite-loop" aria-label="Permalink to “How mlopt works — syntax, structure, and the rewrite loop”">​</a></h1><p>The model graph is <strong>syntax</strong>; a category-theoretic <strong>structure</strong> interprets it; the structure&#39;s <strong>axioms</strong> generate rewrites; <strong>search</strong> picks the cheapest; the <strong>certificate</strong> certifies it.</p><h2 id="the-tower" tabindex="-1">The tower <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-tower" aria-label="Permalink to “The tower”">​</a></h2><p>A program is a <strong>1-morphism</strong> between objects (tensor types); a rewrite law is a <strong>2-morphism</strong> between programs; a <em>derivation</em> — one law proven from premises — is a <strong>3-morphism</strong> (a coherence; the machinery measures confluence and divergence between laws). Declared objects — folds, lifts, compositions — are machine-constructed cells written as data. Two games are played on this tower: <strong>search</strong> applies 2-cells inside one equivalence class; <strong>construction</strong> writes new cells (played as episodes on arena boards — see <a href="./the-game.html">the game</a>).</p><h2 id="the-loop-step-by-step" tabindex="-1">The loop, step by step <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-loop-step-by-step" aria-label="Permalink to “The loop, step by step”">​</a></h2><p>Running example: the recurrence <code>h_t = A·h_{t-1} + x_t</code>.</p><ol><li><strong>Syntax</strong> — the graph as written: ops and wires, no meaning.</li><li><strong>Structure</strong> — a signature (the <em>shape</em>: &quot;a fold over <code>(A, b)</code> pairs&quot;) plus axioms (the equations it obeys: associativity + unit). <em>Example: the monoid <code>aff(A, b)</code>.</em></li><li><strong>Match (the lift)</strong> — recognize that a piece of the graph has the signature&#39;s shape. Matching runs on the <strong>signature</strong>, never the axioms.</li><li><strong>Rewrite</strong> — the axioms become rewrite rules. Side conditions (<code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> hooks) are the soundness boundary.</li><li><strong>Saturate</strong> — apply the axioms to a fixed point. The e-graph <em>is</em> this closure — the consequences, not a search over them.</li><li><strong>Extract</strong> — pick the cheapest program the backend can run. <em>Example: the Blelloch tree.</em></li><li><strong>Certify</strong> — the path original → extracted is a replayable certificate (a 2-cell).</li><li><strong>Evaluate</strong> — describe and price the extracted program. Evaluation <em>ranks</em>; it never changes what is equivalent.</li></ol><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>syntax → structure(signature + axioms) → match(signature)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>       → rewrite(axioms) → saturate → extract → certify → evaluate</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If the structure names the target directly, <strong>construct</strong> it and skip saturate/extract.</p><h2 id="two-consequences-worth-stating" tabindex="-1">Two consequences worth stating <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-consequences-worth-stating" aria-label="Permalink to “Two consequences worth stating”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Laws are entailed by the structure</strong>, in two tiers: <em>axioms</em> (defining equations) and <em>consequences</em> (theorems derived from them). The split is measured on the shipped library, not asserted — <code>rule.kind</code> marks each member <code>axiom</code>/<code>lemma</code>/<code>redundant</code>, and <code>rule.derivation</code> records the premises proving each non-axiom.</li><li><strong>Choosing the interpretation is the discovery act.</strong> The same graph reads as plain composition, a comonoid, a low-rank approximation, or a contraction problem — each exposing different laws.</li></ul><h2 id="the-meta-layer-—-laws-about-laws" tabindex="-1">The meta-layer — laws about laws <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-meta-layer-—-laws-about-laws" aria-label="Permalink to “The meta-layer — laws about laws”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Coherence (3-cells).</strong> <code>mlopt_discovery.coherence</code>/<code>coherence2</code> measure the pair layer over the shipped library: critical pairs, derivability edges, mediators. The derived layer is <em>flat</em> — every derivation is a self-supporting cycle plus one emergent law — and &quot;derivable&quot; is not &quot;disposable&quot;: removing a derivable law costs real bounded reach.</li><li><strong>Discovery (the closed loop).</strong> <code>mlopt_discovery.pipeline</code> runs census → propose → verify → measure → rank → emit over a corpus of real exported models; adversarial witness generation (<code>gap_gen</code>) produces bindings the corpus cannot, and an sqlite evidence store caches verdicts keyed by corpus × rules × revision.</li><li><strong>Minting (ADR-0007).</strong> Four layers grow the object language itself: definitional atoms (anti-unification), handler-interpreted atoms, law-theoretic atoms, and fenced induced atoms — all under one admission <a href="./the-gauntlet.html">gauntlet</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="the-kernel-and-the-platform-next-door" tabindex="-1">The kernel, and the platform next door <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-kernel-and-the-platform-next-door" aria-label="Permalink to “The kernel, and the platform next door”">​</a></h2><p>The same calculus is implemented as a <em>language</em> by the handl platform: laws emit as <code>TransformDef</code> data rows, handl&#39;s rewrite engine fires them, and a mlopt law produces exactly the term mlopt&#39;s own <code>EGraph.apply_rule</code> produces. The seam is <strong>data in, terms out</strong> — the <code>mlopt:</code> extension field on every row keeps the two representations from drifting silently.</p><h2 id="where-this-lives" tabindex="-1">Where this lives <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-this-lives" aria-label="Permalink to “Where this lives”">​</a></h2><p>In <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/eqsat" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>eqsat</code></a>: <code>mlopt_core</code> (IR, <code>EGraph</code>, laws, <code>verify_certificate</code>, cost models) · <code>mlopt_orchestrator</code> (<code>Optimizer</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>lower</code>) — pinned here as git deps. In this repo: <code>mlopt_torch</code> / <code>mlopt_onnx</code> (the <code>Source</code>/<code>Sink</code> ports) · <code>mlopt_discovery</code> (the meta-layer) · <code>bench/drivers/</code> (measurement probes).</p><p>The full account — with the measured asides — is <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/graphopt/blob/main/docs/mechanism.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>docs/mechanism.md</code></a> upstream. See also <a href="./evaluation.html">evaluation</a> and <a href="./three-costs.html">the three costs</a>.</p>`,18)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};